> The mistake was to report an inference as a fact. A failed require
> showed only that the load failed, and I named a cause without
> checking it. Patch 2 of the same series adds a rule to the
> dependencies section of CLAUDE.md: before calling a package missing,
> ask pkg with "pkg info -x <name>" or "pkg which <file>", and report
> what pkg says. Both commands work in this jail; pkg which names
> notmuch-0.40_1 as the owner of /usr/local/bin/notmuch.

That's good but I think it reveals another principle: LLM needs to
discern inference and facts. When it makes claims, it should look for
opportunities to verify it, i.e. uncover and report a fact. If a claim
is inference, that should be noted as well.

In other words, claims should be clearly indicated as facts or
inference, with evidence to back up each. Is that a reasonable
instruction that will result in better outcomes? I'm interested to see
if there's any prior art on that sort of thing.
